  "exploiter_output": {
    "evidence": "Root cause / sink: ScriptStringLookup.lookup(String key) at src/main/java/org/apache/commons/text/lookup/ScriptStringLookup.java:69, specifically scriptEngine.eval(script) at line 86. Trust boundary crossed: any string passed to StringSubstitutor.replace()/StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator() \u2014 e.g. log messages, HTTP headers, config templates, user-supplied text \u2014 is treated as a trusted template and its \"${script:EngineName:script-body}\" tokens are evaluated with a live javax.script.ScriptEngine (Nashorn), giving full Java interop (Runtime.exec, reflection, file I/O). Verified in this checkout: StringLookupFactory.addDefaultStringLookups() (StringLookupFactory.java:407-416) unconditionally registers every DefaultStringLookup enum value \u2014 including SCRIPT (KEY_SCRIPT=\"script\"), DNS (KEY_DNS=\"dns\") and URL (KEY_URL=\"url\") \u2014 into the map used by the singleton InterpolatorStringLookup.INSTANCE returned by StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.interpolatorStringLookup() (line 706-708), which is exactly what StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator() (StringSubstitutor.java:303-304) uses as its default lookup. I confirmed by temporarily excluding SCRIPT/DNS/URL from that loop that the PoV's marker-file side effect disappears (exit 1), then reverted the change. Every alert trace (all 4 provided) funnels through the same StringSubstitutor.substitute()/resolveVariable() internals (lines ~974-1433) into this one sink \u2014 they differ only in which StringSubstitutor entry point/overload was used to build the source TextStringBuilder/StringBuilder (replace(String), replace(StringBuilder), replace(CharSequence,...) etc.), not in root cause. A complete fix must guard every path that can reach ScriptStringLookup.lookup(), which in this codebase means: (1) the \"script\" key registered by StringLookupFactory.addDefaultStringLookups()/DefaultStringLookup.SCRIPT and reachable via StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.interpolatorStringLookup() / StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator(); (2) the \"dns\" key (DefaultStringLookup.DNS -> dnsStringLookup(), can trigger SSRF/DNS-based side effects) registered the same way; (3) the \"url\" key (DefaultStringLookup.URL -> urlStringLookup(), can trigger SSRF) registered the same way; (4) direct programmatic use of StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.scriptStringLookup() or ScriptStringLookup.INSTANCE.lookup(key) bypassing the interpolator map entirely \u2014 a fix that only changes the default map (defense at the interpolator layer) does not stop code that explicitly requests scriptStringLookup(), so any policy/gating should also be enforced at ScriptStringLookup.lookup() itself or documented as an intentionally-explicit, non-default API.",
    "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "On unpatched code, StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator().replace() resolves the \"${script:javascript:...}\" token in the attacker-controlled input, invokes ScriptEngine.eval() (Nashorn), which runs `java.lang.Runtime.getRuntime().exec(['/bin/sh','-c','touch /tmp/text4shell_pov_marker.txt']).waitFor()`. The OS process actually runs and creates /tmp/text4shell_pov_marker.txt, proving real code/command execution (not just string interpolation). The PoV detects the marker file, prints \"VULNERABLE: ...\", deletes the marker, and exits 0. After a correct fix (script/dns/url lookups removed from the default/opt-out interpolator, or ScriptStringLookup disabled/gated), the \"${script:...}\" token is either left unresolved, throws, or errors \u2014 the marker file is never created, the PoV prints \"NOT VULNERABLE: ...\" and exits 1.",
    "notes": "Targeted alert traces 1 and 2 (StringSubstitutor.replace(String) at lines 1036/1040 and 1015/1019, flowing through substitute()/resolveVariable() at 1307-1433 into ScriptStringLookup.lookup()/scriptEngine.eval() at ScriptStringLookup.java:69-86) because that is the most direct, most commonly-used public entry point (StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator().replace(String)) and cleanly demonstrates the shared root cause. Traces 3 and 4 (StringBuilder/TextStringBuilder-based replace(StringBuilder)/replace(CharSequence,...) overloads at lines 974-1000) converge on the exact same substitute()/resolveVariable()->ScriptStringLookup.lookup() code path (lines 1307 onward are identical in all four traces), so they share the identical root cause and the same fix location; they are not separately exercised by this PoV but are covered by the same guard described in regression_hint. I verified the PoV is a true positive/negative discriminator by temporarily patching StringLookupFactory.addDefaultStringLookups() in this worktree to skip SCRIPT/DNS/URL (simulating the real upstream fix), confirming the PoV then exits 1 with \"NOT VULNERABLE\", and immediately reverting that change (git status shows only the untracked .security-pipeline/ directory remains \u2014 no product code diff).",
    "pov_command": "cd /workspace/repo && mvn -B -q -DskipTests -Drat.skip=true package && mvn -B -q -Drat.skip=true dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=/tmp/cp.txt && CP=\"target/classes:$(cat /tmp/cp.txt)\" && mkdir -p /tmp/pov-classes && javac -cp \"$CP\" -d /tmp/pov-classes .security-pipeline/pov/Text4ShellPov.java && java -cp \"/tmp/pov-classes:$CP\" Text4ShellPov",
    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/Text4ShellPov.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce: StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator() / StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.interpolatorStringLookup() (the \"default\", no-argument, opt-out entry points) must NOT resolve \"script\", \"dns\", or \"url\" lookup prefixes against attacker-influenced template text unless the caller has explicitly opted in (e.g. via interpolatorStringLookup(Set<DefaultStringLookup>) or an explicit allow-list/EnumSet parameter naming those lookups). Concretely: after the fix, `StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator().replace(\"${script:javascript:...}\")` should leave the token unresolved/return it unchanged (or throw a well-defined exception) rather than invoking ScriptEngine.eval(); likewise for `${dns:...}` and `${url:...}`. Known bypass variants the fix must also block / that a patch review should re-check: (1) case/whitespace variants of the prefix \u2014 InterpolatorStringLookup.toKey() lower-cases keys, so \"Script:\", \"SCRIPT:\", \" script :\" must still be blocked, not just exact-case \"script\"; (2) the split-on-first-colon parsing in ScriptStringLookup.lookup() (key.split(SPLIT_STR, 2)) \u2014 engine names other than \"javascript\"/\"JavaScript\" (e.g. \"nashorn\", \"js\", \"graal.js\" if present on the classpath) must be equally blocked, since the guard should be at the lookup-registration/dispatch level, not by engine name; (3) nested/recursive interpolation \u2014 a value pulled from another enabled lookup (e.g. \"sys\", \"env\", \"properties\") that itself contains \"${script:...}\" and gets re-substituted must not re-enable script execution; (4) programmatic bypass \u2014 code that calls StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.scriptStringLookup() or the package-private ScriptStringLookup.INSTANCE directly, or builds a custom StringLookup map still containing \"script\"/\"dns\"/\"url\" via addDefaultStringLookups(), must be covered by documentation/deprecation or a runtime guard, since it circumvents an interpolator-only fix; (5) the sibling \"dns\" and \"url\" lookups (dnsStringLookup(), urlStringLookup()) must be removed from the default map in the same fix \u2014 a patch that only touches ScriptStringLookup/the \"script\" key but leaves \"dns\"/\"url\" defaulted-on is incomplete (SSRF-class risk). Regression test should assert both: unpatched-style default interpolator no longer executes ${script:...}, AND explicit opt-in (if the fix adds one) still allows ${script:...} to work when a caller deliberately requests it.",
